4.2.2
Regularization through the variational marginalization of a latent variable
Another way to control the information content in the case of latent variable models is to
replace the latent variable z with a noisy random variable, z ∼q(z | y). Then, instead of
energy minimization, one minimizes the expected value of the energy:
〈F(y)〉z =
Z
dzq(z | y)E(y,z) dz.
The information content of q(z | y) can be measured using the Kullback-Leibler (KL) diver-
gence KL(q(z | y) || p(z)), where p(z) is the prior Ey[q(z | y)]. The KL divergence measures
the level of dependence of q(z | y) on y. So, we can recast the regularized AE as jointly
minimizing the expected energy while also maximizing the entropy.
5
Examples of energy-based models
In this section, we describe a few examples of EBMs of historical and practical relevance. We
start with the Hopfield networks and Boltzmann machines and focus on denoising and masking
AEs in the end.
5.1
Hopfield networks
Hopfield networks are fully-connected recurrent networks popularized by John Hopfield in
1982 [48]. They have symmetric connections, wi j = w ji, and binary activations, yi ∈{−1,1}).
The scheme of such a network is presented in Fig. 7(a). It has the following resulting energy
function:
Fw(y) = −
