This training rule is called Hebbian learning by neuroscientists. It states: whenever two neu-
rons are active simultaneously, strengthen the connection between them. The problem of such
training is the lack of contrastive terms which contributes to the emergence of the so-called
spurious minima (or memories), i.e., accidental energy minima not shaped by the training
data which may be found during inference. This limitation renders the Hopfield networks less
usable in practice.
